<span class="dilemma__note sticky" style="--tilt:4deg">Decision Lab</span><p class="dilemma__eyebrow">Fictional case · Biotech AI</p><p class="lab-disclosure">An illustration of my reasoning, not client work. Company, discovery, budgets and outcomes are hypothetical; linked research is identified separately.</p><div class="lab-slides" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Decision Lab slide content"><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="1 of 8"><p class="lab-step">The brief</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">“How much investment<br>do you need?”</h2><blockquote>“Build an AI assistant for our scientists. We have enough data scientists, little software engineering expertise, and I want us to be an AI frontrunner.”</blockquote><p>Our fictional CEO is the HiPPO: the highest-paid person’s opinion carries weight. There is no agreed budget. I take the ambition seriously—and ask which research decision we need to accelerate.</p><p class="lab-takeaway">2,000 employees, including 600 scientists. Scattered lab records, reports and departmental knowledge. The problem is access to evidence, not a shortage of models.</p></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="2 of 8"><p class="lab-step">Your decision</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">What would you do first?</h2><p>Choose a response to the CEO. Each has a case—and a consequence.</p><p class="lab-answer-hint" id="lab-answer-hint">Select one answer. Double-click the selected answer to clear it.</p><div class="lab-answers" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Your response to the CEO" aria-describedby="lab-answer-hint">
<button type="button" data-answer="build" role="radio" aria-checked="false"><span class="lab-radio" aria-hidden="true"></span>Build our own assistant to lead the market</button>
<button type="button" data-answer="buy" role="radio" aria-checked="false"><span class="lab-radio" aria-hidden="true"></span>Buy a proven assistant and move quickly</button>
<button type="button" data-answer="test" role="radio" aria-checked="false"><span class="lab-radio" aria-hidden="true"></span>Compare a narrow pilot with better search</button></div>
<div class="lab-feedback"><p class="lab-assessment" role="status" aria-live="polite">Choose an answer to see my assessment.</p><div class="lab-choice-details"></div></div>
<noscript><p>A: control, but engineering risk. B: speed, but connector and fit risk. C: my starting choice; it must be time-boxed to avoid delaying useful delivery.</p></noscript></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="3 of 8"><p class="lab-step">Evidence & assumptions</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Size the problem.<br>Don’t invent an average.</h2><p class="lab-slide-summary">Recent research points to fragmented lab knowledge. For this fictional company, 5–7 hours of search and collection per scientist per week remains an assumption to test.</p><details class="lab-more"><summary>Read the research, sources and limitations</summary><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>2025 research · scattered lab knowledge</h3><p><a href="https://pistoiaalliance.org/news/survey-ai-adoption-life-sciences-labs-skills-gap/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pistoia Alliance · September 2025 ↗</a>: 57% cited data silos as a barrier to making best use of lab data. Survey of 200+ experts across life sciences and related sectors; not a representative sample of 2,000-person biotechs.</p></div>
<div class="dilemma__option"><h3>2024 research · a fragmented software estate</h3><p><a href="https://www.benchling.com/blog/2024-state-of-tech-report" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Benchling · November 2024 ↗</a>: 43% of IT respondents at large biopharma supported more than 20 scientific applications. The survey covered 300 R&D/IT respondents in the US and Europe; “large” meant 1,000+ employees. Vendor-sponsored, self-reported evidence.</p></div>
<div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Scenario assumption · 5–7 hours/week</h3><p>Use this search-and-collection range for the fictional company, with 6h as the working case. These recent studies support the fragmentation problem; they do not establish a scientist-specific weekly search average. Measure our baseline before treating it as an investment case.</p></div>
</details></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="4 of 8"><p class="lab-step">Discovery & experiment</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Find the friction<br>before funding the assistant.</h2><p class="lab-slide-summary">Start with one candidate-review workflow, 30 committed scientists and two approved data sources. Compare AI with improved search over eight weeks.</p><details class="lab-more"><summary>Explore discovery, pilot design and €80–150k cost</summary><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Proposed discovery—not completed findings</h3><p>Observe 12 scientists across three departments; log two weeks of search, waiting, synthesis and correction separately. Trace ELN/LIMS, file shares and expert handoffs. Test whether access rights, metadata or missing records cause the delay.</p></div><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>An 8-week evidence-generating pilot</h3><p>30 committed scientists, one candidate-review brief, two approved data sources. Compare current workflow, improved non-AI search, and source-linked AI on matched tasks in randomized order. Include difficult and unanswerable questions; scientists score usefulness blind where practical.</p></div><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Proposed first tranche · €80–150k</h3><p>Planning range: 4–5 FTE-equivalent × 2 months × €10–14k/FTE-month, plus €0–10k tools. Product/domain lead, senior integration engineer, data engineer, data scientist/evaluation lead and fractional security/UX/scientist time. Secure engineering capacity before starting; adequate data science does not replace it.</p></div></details></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="5 of 8"><p class="lab-step">Market scan</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Buy, adapt or build?</h2><p class="lab-slide-summary">Compare Microsoft 365 Copilot, Glean and Benchling AI with an open-source hybrid and a custom build. Existing systems and engineering capacity should drive the shortlist.</p><details class="lab-more"><summary>Compare products, costs and team capabilities</summary><p class="lab-source">Live product documentation and pricing rechecked 28 October 2026 (access date, not study date). Capabilities are vendor claims; test against the same evidence set. Costs below are estimates unless marked published.</p><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Microsoft 365 Copilot · existing M365 estate</h3><p><a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/pricing/enterprise" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Published: US$30/user/month, annual ↗</a>; 600 seats = US$216k/year, plus qualifying M365 licenses. Good fit for office knowledge; prove ELN/LIMS coverage and permissions. Illustrative 2–3 internal FTE + €30–80k services/usage: €270–584k/year, PLUS USD licenses; no currency conversion assumed.</p></div><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Glean · cross-system retrieval</h3><p><a href="https://docs.glean.com/connectors/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Permissions-aware connectors ↗</a>; prove scientific source parsing and freshness. No verified public list price in this research: obtain a quote. Illustrative 2–3 FTE + €30–80k services = €270–584k/year PLUS vendor quote. Better breadth is useful only if our lab systems connect.</p></div><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Benchling AI · lab-native option</h3><p><a href="https://www.benchling.com/ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Search and answers over Benchling R&D data ↗</a>. Stronger fit if records already live there; migration and outside-system coverage can dominate. Obtain platform/AI/usage quote. Illustrative 2–3 FTE + €30–100k integration = €270–604k/year PLUS quote and any migration.</p></div><details><summary>Compare open-source/hybrid and in-house</summary><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Haystack + hosted model · 4–6 FTE</h3><p><a href="https://docs.haystack.deepset.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open-source retrieval pipelines ↗</a>; control over retrieval and evaluations. We own connectors, access enforcement and operations. €480k–1.01m staff + €40–120k infrastructure/API = ~€520k–1.13m first year. Needs senior backend/platform, data integration, retrieval/evaluation, product/domain and fractional security/UX.</p></div><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Custom in-house application · 7–10 FTE</h3><p>~€840k–1.68m staff + €80–200k infrastructure/API = ~€920k–1.88m first year. Adds frontend, QA and reliability capacity. Uses existing models; training a foundation model is excluded. Highest engineering burden and slowest fit to current capabilities.</p></div></details><p class="lab-takeaway">Cost method: €120–168k loaded cost per FTE-year; scenario assumptions, not market averages or supplier quotes. Includes allocated internal time; excludes VAT, major data remediation and whole-platform replacement. Year-two staffing may remain similar. Buy also needs an accountable internal team. Shortlist by existing estate, then price equivalent scope.</p></details></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="6 of 8"><p class="lab-step">Value today</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Capacity is not cash.</h2><p>Illustrative scale model—not pilot results. The 5–7h baseline and 15–35% reduction are assumptions to test, not averages from the cited studies. Adjust the scenario to see how much the decision depends on adoption and correction effort.</p><label for="lab-scenario">Planning scenario</label><select id="lab-scenario"><option value="low">Cautious</option><option value="base" selected>Working assumption</option><option value="high">Upside</option></select><div id="lab-model" aria-live="polite"></div><details><summary>Show the calculation and limits</summary><p>600 scientists × 46 working weeks × adoption × (search hours/week × reduction − added review hours/week). Cautious: 40%, 5h, 15%, 0.5h; working: 60%, 6h, 25%, 0.5h; upside: 80%, 7h, 35%, 0.4h.</p><p>At an assumed €70/hour, gross capacity value is ~€0.2m / €1.2m / €3.2m annually, before ongoing costs. This is opportunity cost, not payroll savings, revenue or a financial return. No released budget without an explicit redeployment or spending decision. A failed pilot could show zero or negative net benefit.</p></details><p class="lab-takeaway">Validate search time, sustained adoption and correction cost in the pilot. Do not multiply a best-case task demo across all 2,000 employees.</p></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="7 of 8"><p class="lab-step">Future product line</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Can we learn faster<br>the second time?</h2><p class="lab-slide-summary">A new product line tests whether the investment creates reusable capabilities—not just time savings today. Both scenarios below are assumptions, with zero milestone gain possible.</p><details class="lab-more"><summary>Explore capability reuse and innovation timelines</summary><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Assumption · a new product line, 100 scientists</h3><p>Reusable source connectors, access rules, evidence templates and evaluation sets could shorten knowledge onboarding. New scientific entities, data formats or permissions may eliminate that advantage.</p></div><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Capability lead time · 16 → 10–14 weeks</h3><p>Illustrative baseline: 16 weeks to deliver a usable evidence workflow from scratch. Reuse could remove 2–6 weeks (~15–40% shorter delivery time, rounded). This is a hypothesis about capability delivery, not drug-development speed.</p></div><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Research milestone · 24-week plan</h3><p>If evidence preparation is 4 weeks on the critical path, a 25–50% reduction saves 1–2 weeks (~4–8% elapsed time). If wet-lab work or approvals dominate, milestone gain is zero. More parallel capacity does not automatically move the critical path.</p></div><p class="lab-takeaway">Track time to first trustworthy evidence brief, reuse effort and time to a candidate decision. Run a second-team transfer test. Do not add these milestone weeks to the annual capacity value: the same benefit may be counted twice.</p></details></article><article class="lab-slide" aria-label="8 of 8"><p class="lab-step">My recommendation</p><h2 class="dilemma__title">Fund learning.<br>Earn the next commitment.</h2><p class="lab-slide-summary">Request €80–150k for a bounded pilot. Secure engineering capacity, approved data access and committed scientists before starting; use the evidence to decide what earns more investment.</p><details class="lab-more"><summary>Read funding gates and team commitments</summary><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Answer to the CEO</h3><p>Request €80–150k for the scoped pilot, conditional on approved access, a senior integration engineer and 30 committed scientists. Prioritize an existing-estate vendor against improved search; use a hybrid only where a demonstrated gap justifies ownership. No blank cheque for a platform.</p></div><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Pre-agreed gates—not reported outcomes</h3><p>Aim for ≥20% net preparation-time reduction against improved search, with no material quality loss. Require verifiable support for material claims and no unauthorized disclosure in adversarial tests. Report task/user spread and uncertainty; inconclusive evidence earns a narrower test, not a rollout.</p></div><div class="dilemma__option"><h3>Make collaboration a commitment</h3><p>Scientists own usefulness and review; data owners own access and metadata; engineering owns supportability; finance challenges capacity assumptions. CEO sponsors the next decision, not the preferred answer. Review at week eight; stop if review effort erases gains.</p></div></details></article></div><nav class="lab-controls" aria-label="Decision Lab slides" hidden><button type="button" class="lab-prev" aria-label="Previous slide">←</button><span class="lab-count" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></span><button type="button" class="lab-next">Next →</button></nav>
</aside>